[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"CI/CD Pipeline Integration for Postman API Testing"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Automating API tests is a crucial step in modern software development. By integrating Postman with CI/CD platforms like Jenkins, GitHub Actions, and GitLab CI, you can execute your Postman collections automatically on every code commit or pull request using Newman, the command-line collection runner for Postman."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A Postman Collection and associated Environment exported as JSON files."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node.js and npm installed on your local machine or CI/CD runner."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Access to a Jenkins server, a GitHub repository, or a GitLab repository."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Installing Newman Locally"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before configuring your CI/CD pipelines, you need Newman installed to run your collections via the command line. Run the following command in your terminal:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"npm install -g newman"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: GitHub Actions Integration"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"GitHub Actions allows you to build CI/CD workflows directly in your GitHub repository. Create a file named .github/workflows/postman-tests.yml in your repository with the following configuration:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"name: Postman API Tests
on: [push]
jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v3
      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v3
        with:
          node-version: '18'
      - name: Install Newman
        run: npm install -g newman
      - name: Run Postman Collection
        run: newman run "./tests/collection.json" -e "./tests/environment.json""},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: GitLab CI Integration"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"For GitLab, you can define your pipeline using a .gitlab-ci.yml file in the root of your project directory. Add the following stages to execute your API tests:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"stages:
  - test

postman_api_test:
  stage: test
  image: node:18
  script:
    - npm install -g newman
    - newman run "tests/collection.json" -e "tests/environment.json""},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Jenkins Pipeline Integration"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Jenkins, you can use a Declarative Pipeline script (Jenkinsfile) to run Newman. Ensure Node.js is installed on your Jenkins agent and configured under Global Tool Configuration."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                checkout scm
            }
        }
        stage('Install Dependencies') {
            steps {
                sh 'npm install -g newman'
            }
        }
        stage('Run API Tests') {
            steps {
                sh 'newman run tests/collection.json -e tests/environment.json'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"You have successfully integrated Postman API testing into GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and Jenkins. This ensures your APIs are continuously tested against functional and regression bugs on every code change."}]
